---
// The privacy page, in the same look as the 404. Its words are the owner's,
// drafted from ooer.com's own and changed only where this site differs, and
// are reviewed by date: the date in the last paragraph is the one to move
// when they are read again. Without it the site would say nothing of what it
// keeps or where a request goes, and the footer would link nowhere.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import OutsideLink from '../components/OutsideLink.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Privacy: ooerflix"
  description="What this site collects, where it goes, and what is kept in your browser."
  skipLabel="Skip to the content"
  indexed
>
  <div class="prose">
    <h1>Privacy</h1>
    <p>
      No analytics, no trackers, no cookies. The longer version follows, because
      the short one is the part everybody claims.
    </p>

    <h2>What this site collects</h2>
    <p>
      Nothing. There is no analytics script, no tag manager, no pixel, no
      embedded video or font calling home, and no advertising. Every asset the
      page loads, its styles, its one script, its two typefaces and its
      pictures, comes from this domain. That is not a claim you have to take on
      trust: the content policy this site sends refuses anything from anywhere
      else, so a third-party request would not merely be absent, it would be
      blocked.
    </p>
    <p>
      There is no account to make, no newsletter to join and no form. Nothing on
      the site asks you for anything.
    </p>

    <h2>What your browser keeps</h2>
    <p>
      Nothing. The site sets no cookie and stores nothing in your browser: there
      is no setting to remember.
    </p>
    <p>
      The page asks your browser whether it can run WebGPU, so that a card can
      say when a game will not run in it; the answer is not kept or sent
      anywhere.
    </p>

    <h2>What the host sees</h2>
    <p>
      This is a static site on{' '}
      <OutsideLink href="https://www.netlify.com/">Netlify</OutsideLink>, and a
      server that answers a request necessarily sees the request: your IP
      address, the page asked for, the time, and the user agent string your
      browser sends. That is a web server log. It is not something this site
      switches on, and it is not something a site can switch off: it is the
      shape of the protocol. Netlify's own{' '}
      <OutsideLink href="https://www.netlify.com/privacy/">
        privacy policy
      </OutsideLink>{' '}
      covers what it does with that.
    </p>
    <p>
      Nothing in this site's own code reads, forwards, joins or stores any of
      it, and no log is exported anywhere.
    </p>

    <h2>Where the cards go</h2>
    <p>
      A card that says Play now is a link to the game itself, and the game is
      another site with a host of its own, which sees your request to it just as
      this site's host sees a request here. Some of the games are served from
      GitHub Pages, and GitHub's{' '}
      <OutsideLink href="https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement">
        privacy statement
      </OutsideLink>{' '}
      covers those. What a game keeps in your browser is for the game to say:
      this page speaks only for this site.
    </p>

    <h2>Getting in touch</h2>
    <p>
      There is no contact form and no mailing list, which is the same as saying
      there is no address here to give away. If something needs saying, the{' '}
      <OutsideLink href="https://github.com/onion2k/flix/issues">
        issue tracker
      </OutsideLink>{' '}
      is read.
    </p>

    <p>
      Last reviewed <strong>22 November 2026</strong>. If this page ever stops
      being true, that is a bug, and the tracker above is where it goes.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
